1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,155
A voice recording goes in on the left.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:07,226
Five programs, each in its own container,
turn it into a podcast in English

3
00:00:07,226 --> 00:00:10,837
and in French,
without ever sending each other a message.

4
00:00:11,157 --> 00:00:13,547
So how do they cooperate, and who pays?

5
00:00:13,783 --> 00:00:16,221
Since last lecture, four things change:

6
00:00:16,221 --> 00:00:20,034
you write the Dockerfile,
and you build five images, not one.

7
00:00:20,354 --> 00:00:23,183
A bind mount opens a window
into your laptop,

8
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:27,018
and the containers reach Google Cloud
through a shared bucket

9
00:00:27,018 --> 00:00:28,653
and a service account key.

10
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,143
Open the mega-pipeline
README beside this video.

11
00:00:32,463 --> 00:00:36,549
The tracker, top left, splits the README
into numbered steps;

12
00:00:36,549 --> 00:00:39,161
code panels show file and line numbers.

13
00:00:39,481 --> 00:00:41,845
Pause at the yellow your-turn tag;

14
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:45,391
a gray extra tag marks
a command not in the README.

15
00:00:45,633 --> 00:00:48,833
You'll need Tutorial 0,
for Docker Desktop and uv,

16
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:51,905
the course key from Canvas,
and your group name.

17
00:00:52,225 --> 00:00:56,364
Every command is also in the study guide,
linked in the description.

18
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,666
One honest limit:
we recorded without a course key,

19
00:00:59,666 --> 00:01:01,890
so wherever the code talks to Google,

20
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:05,496
you'll see the command and what to look for,
not its output.

21
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:09,892
We recorded on a Debian 12 laptop
with Docker Engine 29,

22
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:11,749
at the time of recording.

23
00:01:12,069 --> 00:01:17,157
On a Mac or Windows, the prompt differs
and Docker Desktop runs the containers;

24
00:01:17,157 --> 00:01:18,832
the commands are the same.

25
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:23,110
In production,
services call each other's APIs.

26
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:25,040
This tutorial simplifies:

27
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,326
each component writes its output
to shared storage,

28
00:01:28,326 --> 00:01:30,107
where the next one reads it.

29
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:35,598
That storage is one Google Cloud Storage
bucket, drawn as a cabinet of drawers.

30
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:37,218
The first stage, A,

31
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:41,870
turns recordings from input audios
into transcripts in text prompts.

32
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:46,094
Then B turns each transcript
into a longer podcast script,

33
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:47,374
in text paragraphs.

34
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:49,132
From there the path splits.

35
00:01:49,452 --> 00:01:54,444
C turns the script into English speech,
stored in the drawer named text audios.

36
00:01:54,764 --> 00:01:57,938
D translates it into French,
in text translated,

37
00:01:57,938 --> 00:02:00,716
and E speaks the French, in output audios.

38
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,603
Nothing triggers anything:

39
00:02:02,603 --> 00:02:05,502
you run each component yourself,
in this order.

40
00:02:05,784 --> 00:02:13,609
A bucket has no real folders:
a name like text_prompts/group-01/input-…

41
00:02:13,609 --> 00:02:17,026
.txt is one single object name.

42
00:02:17,267 --> 00:02:21,021
Here's A's upload code:
prefix, group name, file name,

43
00:02:21,021 --> 00:02:23,454
and only files starting with input.

44
00:02:23,774 --> 00:02:28,766
B downloads with the same pattern:
that naming contract is the whole wiring.

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,007
The group name keeps teams apart,
and its default has no guard:

46
00:02:33,007 --> 00:02:37,460
a component you forgot to edit writes
into the default group's drawer,

47
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:39,432
maybe over someone else's work.

48
00:02:39,752 --> 00:02:41,288
Set it before every run.

49
00:02:41,534 --> 00:02:45,310
Each stage that succeeds lights up your row
on a live leaderboard.

50
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:50,088
Every call and every byte is billed
to the course's project, not yours.

51
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:54,387
Which two stages could run at the same time,
and which one has to wait?

52
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:08,482
C and D both read only B's text paragraphs,
so they could run side by side.

53
00:03:08,802 --> 00:03:11,554
E reads what D writes, so it waits for D.

54
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,416
The README's first command: clone the repo.

55
00:03:15,736 --> 00:03:20,237
Type git clone and the address,
copied from the README, and pause to run it.

56
00:03:20,483 --> 00:03:22,254
Then cd mega-pipeline.

57
00:03:22,574 --> 00:03:27,350
An extra check, not in the README,
lists the files and shows the commit:

58
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:32,259
five component folders, on the main branch,
at the commit this video uses.

59
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,889
Another extra lists all five folders.

60
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:39,289
Each has a README, its own cli.py,
and a secrets folder,

61
00:03:39,289 --> 00:03:44,681
and not one has a Dockerfile:
the README says you write all five yourself.

62
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:46,560
Two differences to remember.

63
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:51,779
The generate text folder already
has its own pyproject.toml

64
00:03:51,779 --> 00:03:53,356
and a main.py file,

65
00:03:53,356 --> 00:03:55,242
but no .gitignore file.

66
00:03:55,562 --> 00:03:58,037
And synthesis audio has a bonus script.

67
00:03:58,283 --> 00:03:59,862
Now the first component.

68
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:03,430
Open the README
in the transcribe audio folder:

69
00:04:03,430 --> 00:04:07,990
the tracker switches to it,
and its step 1 is cd transcribe_audio.

70
00:04:08,310 --> 00:04:10,017
Pause, and catch up to here.

71
00:04:10,267 --> 00:04:12,614
Open cli.py in your editor.

72
00:04:12,934 --> 00:04:15,297
The first imports come with Python,

73
00:04:15,297 --> 00:04:18,672
but the two Google Cloud modules
and ffmpeg don't.

74
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:20,891
Part 2 shows where they come from.

75
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,716
Next come four settings:

76
00:04:22,716 --> 00:04:26,081
the bucket's name,
the two drawers this stage uses,

77
00:04:26,081 --> 00:04:28,060
and on line 21 the group name,

78
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:31,160
still at its default,
which you'll change soon.

79
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:34,260
At the bottom, main runs up to three verbs,

80
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,651
always in this order:
download, transcribe, upload.

81
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:41,139
Step 2 is the key:

82
00:04:41,139 --> 00:04:44,902
a file from Canvas
called mega-pipeline.json.

83
00:04:45,222 --> 00:04:48,689
It lets your code act
as the course's service account,

84
00:04:48,689 --> 00:04:52,220
a robot Google account,
so guard it like a credit card.

85
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:56,299
Pause, download it,
and put it in this folder's secrets folder,

86
00:04:56,299 --> 00:04:58,610
with a copy in each of the other four.

87
00:04:59,716 --> 00:05:01,231
The name must be exact.

88
00:05:01,551 --> 00:05:05,306
If your browser saved it with a 1
in parentheses, rename it.

89
00:05:05,866 --> 00:05:06,945
A small mistake:

90
00:05:06,945 --> 00:05:10,381
the component READMEs link
to an older Canvas file,

91
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:12,373
so use the main README's link.

92
00:05:12,616 --> 00:05:14,323
Step 3 uses uv.

93
00:05:14,643 --> 00:05:18,753
After Tutorial 0,
uv --version prints a version,

94
00:05:18,753 --> 00:05:20,893
so skip ahead to uv init.

95
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:24,840
This recording laptop had no uv:
command not found.

96
00:05:25,083 --> 00:05:30,065
Otherwise, use the README's installer:
on macOS or Linux,

97
00:05:30,065 --> 00:05:31,396
this curl line,

98
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:35,920
which installed uv 0.13.0
at the time of recording.

99
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:40,528
On Windows, the PowerShell line;
on any system, pip install uv.

100
00:05:40,766 --> 00:05:44,266
Then restart your terminal,
as the installer says,

101
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:47,486
cd back into transcribe audio,
and uv answers.

102
00:05:47,933 --> 00:05:51,261
Now the step itself: uv init --bare.

103
00:05:51,581 --> 00:05:57,896
It writes just one file, pyproject.toml,
for a project named transcribe-audio.

104
00:05:58,216 --> 00:05:59,368
Pause and run it.

105
00:05:59,616 --> 00:06:01,304
An extra look inside:

106
00:06:01,304 --> 00:06:05,240
a name,
a version, an empty list of dependencies,

107
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:08,171
and the minimum Python version, 3.11.

108
00:06:08,416 --> 00:06:13,600
That 3.11 comes from this laptop's Python,
3.11.2.

109
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:17,748
In the image,
uv sync needs a Python in this range,

110
00:06:17,748 --> 00:06:20,299
and 3.12 is in it, so all is well.

111
00:06:20,533 --> 00:06:22,666
Your number depends on your laptop.

112
00:06:22,986 --> 00:06:25,708
If yours says 3.13 or newer,

113
00:06:25,708 --> 00:06:30,666
the image's 3.12 falls outside,
so edit it to 3.12.

114
00:06:30,986 --> 00:06:34,549
That fix isn't in the README;
generate text shows why.

115
00:06:35,883 --> 00:06:38,107
Step 4 is the heart of this lecture:

116
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:42,368
there's no Dockerfile here,
so you write it yourself, piece by piece.

117
00:06:42,616 --> 00:06:47,885
Line 1 starts from the official
Python 3.12 image on Debian 12.

118
00:06:48,205 --> 00:06:51,384
The README names it in its text,
not in a code block.

119
00:06:51,633 --> 00:06:57,492
Line 3 declares a build argument, a variable
that exists only during the build:

120
00:06:57,492 --> 00:07:00,977
the Debian packages
build-essential and ffmpeg.

121
00:07:01,297 --> 00:07:07,036
The Python package
ffmpeg-python just runs the ffmpeg program.

122
00:07:07,283 --> 00:07:10,846
Lines 5 to 10 set six environment variables.

123
00:07:11,166 --> 00:07:12,306
As in lecture 2,

124
00:07:12,306 --> 00:07:15,582
they only write on the config card:
no layers.

125
00:07:15,902 --> 00:07:19,980
The first four:
apt never asks questions, text is Unicode,

126
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:24,691
Python prints at once,
and uv copies files instead of linking them.

127
00:07:24,933 --> 00:07:26,928
Line 9 matters most:

128
00:07:26,928 --> 00:07:33,210
it tells uv to build the venv
in /home/app/.venv, outside /app.

129
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:35,152
Soon, this line saves us.

130
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:39,724
Line 10 tells Google's libraries
where the key is:

131
00:07:39,724 --> 00:07:43,528
secrets/mega-pipeline.json, a relative path.

132
00:07:43,766 --> 00:07:47,115
Lines 12 to 21 are one RUN instruction.

133
00:07:47,435 --> 00:07:51,864
Every line but the last ends in a backslash,
which continues it,

134
00:07:51,864 --> 00:07:55,393
and && chains the commands,
so they make one layer.

135
00:07:55,713 --> 00:07:58,315
It updates Debian, installs our packages,

136
00:07:58,315 --> 00:08:01,723
and deletes the apt package lists
in that same layer,

137
00:08:01,723 --> 00:08:03,073
so they cost nothing.

138
00:08:03,316 --> 00:08:06,153
Then it upgrades pip and installs
uv with it.

139
00:08:06,473 --> 00:08:09,385
Unlike Debian's system Python in lecture 1,

140
00:08:09,385 --> 00:08:13,514
this Python belongs to the image,
so pip may install into it.

141
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:18,948
Last, it creates a user called app,
user ID 1000, who owns /app:

142
00:08:18,948 --> 00:08:23,414
as the README says,
running as root is a security risk.

143
00:08:23,650 --> 00:08:29,154
Lines 23 and 24 switch to that user and make
/app the working folder.

144
00:08:29,474 --> 00:08:33,367
Line 26 copies everything
in this folder into the image;

145
00:08:33,367 --> 00:08:35,660
hold on to that word, everything.

146
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:40,992
Line 27 runs uv sync
to build the venv from pyproject.toml,

147
00:08:40,992 --> 00:08:47,206
and lines 29 and 30 start bash
with the venv activated, as in lecture 2.

148
00:08:50,733 --> 00:08:54,392
The main README's appendix
has a sample Dockerfile,

149
00:08:54,392 --> 00:08:57,978
but its build argument
has curl instead of ffmpeg:

150
00:08:57,978 --> 00:09:00,632
don't paste it into transcribe audio.

151
00:09:00,866 --> 00:09:05,517
Pause now and write it:
a file named Dockerfile, with no extension.

152
00:09:05,837 --> 00:09:07,091
Type the FROM line,

153
00:09:07,091 --> 00:09:10,259
then copy the README's six code
blocks below it,

154
00:09:10,259 --> 00:09:13,185
in order,
with one blank line between blocks,

155
00:09:13,185 --> 00:09:15,266
so your line numbers match ours.

156
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:19,101
First, an extra check: your Dockerfile,

157
00:09:19,101 --> 00:09:22,855
named exactly that,
sits next to pyproject.toml.

158
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:24,977
Step 5 builds the image.

159
00:09:25,297 --> 00:09:30,033
A small gap: the README names it,
transcribe audio, but gives no command.

160
00:09:30,353 --> 00:09:33,191
Here it is:
docker build -t transcribe_audio,

161
00:09:33,191 --> 00:09:36,092
then a dot for this folder,
the build context.

162
00:09:36,333 --> 00:09:37,485
Pause and run it.

163
00:09:37,805 --> 00:09:40,152
Your terminal shows a compact live view

164
00:09:40,152 --> 00:09:43,522
that folds each build step into one line
when it's done.

165
00:09:43,842 --> 00:09:49,794
Here are the full log's landmarks;
to see them yourself, add --progress=plain.

166
00:09:50,114 --> 00:09:55,981
Build step 2 is our big RUN:
apt installs 237 packages,

167
00:09:55,981 --> 00:10:03,128
a 205 megabyte download,
then pip installs uv, all in 46.9 seconds.

168
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:07,442
Build steps 3 and 4 set the working folder
and copy our files.

169
00:10:07,762 --> 00:10:12,609
Build step 5 runs uv sync
with the image's own Python 3.12,

170
00:10:12,609 --> 00:10:14,006
creates the venv,

171
00:10:14,006 --> 00:10:19,601
and resolves 1 package: the project itself,
with no dependencies yet.

172
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:23,800
It ends by naming the image,
with a warning about line 10:

173
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:27,274
Docker flags any variable
whose name sounds secret.

174
00:10:27,594 --> 00:10:31,818
The value is just a path, so it's harmless,
and you'll see it every build.

175
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:33,313
An extra check,

176
00:10:33,313 --> 00:10:37,550
docker images,
says 1.35 gigabytes on this machine.

177
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:43,222
By far the biggest layer is that one RUN,
842 megabytes of compilers,

178
00:10:43,222 --> 00:10:47,875
ffmpeg and their libraries,
while the copied folder is tiny.

179
00:10:48,117 --> 00:10:51,609
And if you skipped uv init,
build step 5 fails,

180
00:10:51,609 --> 00:10:54,730
because there's no pyproject.toml to sync.

181
00:10:54,967 --> 00:10:55,967
Ponder this.

182
00:10:56,268 --> 00:11:00,430
You change only the group name in cli.py
and build again:

183
00:11:00,430 --> 00:11:03,351
which of the five build steps run again?

184
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:13,821
Build steps 4 and 5.

185
00:11:14,141 --> 00:11:17,458
The copied folder changed,
so the copy runs again,

186
00:11:17,458 --> 00:11:19,581
and so does every step after it.

187
00:11:19,901 --> 00:11:22,591
Build steps 2 and 3 come from the cache,

188
00:11:22,591 --> 00:11:26,088
and build step 1
is the base image you already have.

189
00:11:26,408 --> 00:11:28,008
We'll see it in part 2.

190
00:11:29,650 --> 00:11:32,743
Step 6 runs the image, so first, a picture.

191
00:11:33,063 --> 00:11:37,842
The copied files sit in /app;
the venv sits outside it, in /home.

192
00:11:38,162 --> 00:11:42,941
As in lecture 2,
a container adds a thin writable layer,

193
00:11:42,941 --> 00:11:45,330
thrown away on exit by --rm.

194
00:11:45,567 --> 00:11:49,445
-v mounts the current folder,
pwd, onto /app:

195
00:11:49,445 --> 00:11:54,185
it covers the copied files,
so /app is now your folder.

196
00:11:54,433 --> 00:11:57,612
So edits in VS Code show up inside at once.

197
00:11:57,932 --> 00:12:02,773
Anything written under /app goes straight
into your laptop's folder and stays;

198
00:12:02,773 --> 00:12:05,441
anything else lands on the throwaway layer.

199
00:12:05,683 --> 00:12:06,683
Ponder this.

200
00:12:06,984 --> 00:12:09,843
In lecture 2, the venv sat next to the code.

201
00:12:10,163 --> 00:12:11,465
If it sat in /app here,

202
00:12:11,465 --> 00:12:14,409
what would the container
find there with this mount?

203
00:12:23,250 --> 00:12:25,331
It would find your laptop's folder,

204
00:12:25,331 --> 00:12:28,007
with no venv in it,
so activation would fail.

205
00:12:28,327 --> 00:12:32,082
That's what line 9,
UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT, prevents.

206
00:12:32,317 --> 00:12:38,034
Type docker run, --rm, -ti,
-v with the mount, and the image name.

207
00:12:38,354 --> 00:12:42,066
Copy it from the README, quotes included,
and pause to run it.

208
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:48,233
On Windows, run these commands in Git BASH,
keep the quotes around pwd,

209
00:12:48,233 --> 00:12:50,097
and if docker run says

210
00:12:50,097 --> 00:12:54,417
the input device is not a TTY,
put winpty in front.

211
00:12:54,667 --> 00:12:57,943
The prompt changes:
you're user app, in /app,

212
00:12:57,943 --> 00:13:01,346
and after the @
sign comes the container's ID,

213
00:13:01,346 --> 00:13:02,603
new on every run.

214
00:13:02,850 --> 00:13:07,357
No venv name shows in the prompt,
yet the venv is active:

215
00:13:07,357 --> 00:13:11,469
two extra checks find python in /home,
version 3.12.

216
00:13:11,717 --> 00:13:12,781
Two more extras:

217
00:13:12,781 --> 00:13:15,775
listing the folder
shows your laptop's files,

218
00:13:15,775 --> 00:13:18,458
and the key variable from line 10 is set.

219
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:22,028
Last extra: ask cli.py for its help.

220
00:13:22,348 --> 00:13:27,831
ModuleNotFoundError, No module named google:
the packages aren't installed yet.

221
00:13:28,067 --> 00:13:32,248
The container runs and sees your folder,
but Python can't find Google.

222
00:13:32,568 --> 00:13:35,453
If you stop here, rerun the same docker run

223
00:13:35,453 --> 00:13:38,606
from the transcribe audio
folder before part 2.

224
00:13:38,926 --> 00:13:42,872
In part 2, we add the packages,
and find out why they vanish.

